/-!
# The radical of a preadditive category

The **radical** of a preadditive category is the categorical Jacobson radical: the morphism
`f : X ⟶ Y` lies in `TauCeti.jacobsonRadical X Y` when `𝟙 X - f ≫ g` is invertible for every
`g : Y ⟶ X`. On a single object this is the usual characterization of the Jacobson radical of the
ring `CategoryTheory.End X`, with which it is identified by
`TauCeti.jacobsonRadical_self_eq_jacobson`, and the point of the definition is that the same
formula makes sense
between two different objects and produces a two-sided ideal of the category: a subgroup of each
morphism group, absorbed by composition on either side.

Between objects with **local** endomorphism rings — the indecomposables of a Krull-Schmidt
category — the radical is exactly the set of non-isomorphisms
(`TauCeti.mem_jacobsonRadical_iff_not_isIso`). That is the description under which the radical is
used: for indecomposable `X` and `Y` with `X ≇ Y` every morphism `X ⟶ Y` is radical
(`TauCeti.jacobsonRadical_eq_top`), and inside `X ⟶ X` the radical is the set of non-units of the
local ring `End X` (`TauCeti.mem_jacobsonRadical_self_iff_not_isUnit`). Only the source has to be
indecomposable for a non-split monomorphism to be radical
(`TauCeti.mem_jacobsonRadical_of_not_isSplitMono`), and an irreducible morphism is in particular
radical (`TauCeti.IsIrreducibleMorphism.mem_jacobsonRadical`).

The **square** of the radical, `TauCeti.jacobsonRadicalSq X Y`, is the subgroup generated by the
composites `X ⟶ Z ⟶ Y` of two radical morphisms. It is again a two-sided ideal, contained in the
radical. The quotient `rad(X, Y) / rad²(X, Y)` is what an arrow of the Auslander-Reiten quiver is
a basis vector of; this file supplies the two subgroups, their ideal properties and, over a linear
category, their submodule views, not that quotient.

The engine of the whole development is **Jacobson's lemma** in its categorical form
(`TauCeti.isIso_id_sub_comp_comm`): for `a : X ⟶ Y` and `b : Y ⟶ X`, the endomorphism
`𝟙 X - a ≫ b` of `X` is invertible exactly when `𝟙 Y - b ≫ a` is, by the same formula
`(𝟙 Y - b ≫ a)⁻¹ = 𝟙 Y + b ≫ (𝟙 X - a ≫ b)⁻¹ ≫ a` that proves it for rings. It is what makes
the definition of the radical left-right symmetric, and it is what makes the radical closed under
addition, exactly as in the ring case.

## Main definitions

* `TauCeti.jacobsonRadical X Y`: the radical `rad(X, Y)`, an `AddSubgroup (X ⟶ Y)`.
* `TauCeti.jacobsonRadicalSq X Y`: its square `rad²(X, Y)`.
* `TauCeti.jacobsonRadicalSubmodule k X Y` and `TauCeti.jacobsonRadicalSqSubmodule k X Y`: the
  two of them over a `k`-linear category, as `Submodule k (X ⟶ Y)`.

## Main results

* `TauCeti.isIso_id_sub_comp_comm`: Jacobson's lemma for a preadditive category.
* `TauCeti.mem_jacobsonRadical_iff_isIso_id_sub_comp_left`: the radical is left-right symmetric,
  the defining condition `TauCeti.mem_jacobsonRadical_iff_isIso_id_sub_comp_right` being readable
  with the composite taken in either order.
* `TauCeti.comp_mem_jacobsonRadical_left` and `TauCeti.comp_mem_jacobsonRadical_right`: the
  radical is a **two-sided ideal** of the category.
* `TauCeti.jacobsonRadical_self_eq_jacobson`: on a single object the radical is the **Jacobson
  radical of the endomorphism ring**, `Ring.jacobson (End X)`.
* `TauCeti.mem_jacobsonRadical_iff_not_isIso`: between objects with local endomorphism rings, the
  radical is the set of **non-isomorphisms**; `TauCeti.jacobsonRadical_eq_top` is the case of two
  non-isomorphic such objects, and `TauCeti.mem_jacobsonRadical_self_iff_not_isUnit` the case of
  one object, where it recovers the non-units of `End X`.
* `TauCeti.mem_jacobsonRadical_of_not_isSplitMono`: out of an object with a local endomorphism
  ring, every morphism that is not a split monomorphism is radical; no hypothesis on the target.
* `TauCeti.IsIrreducibleMorphism.mem_jacobsonRadical`: an irreducible morphism out of an object
  with a local endomorphism ring is radical.
* `TauCeti.jacobsonRadicalSq_le_iff`: the universal property of the square of the radical, a
  subgroup contains it exactly when it contains every composite of two radical morphisms.
* `TauCeti.jacobsonRadicalSq_le_jacobsonRadical`,
  `TauCeti.comp_mem_jacobsonRadicalSq_left` and `TauCeti.comp_mem_jacobsonRadicalSq_right`: the
  square of the radical is a two-sided ideal contained in the radical.
* `TauCeti.smul_mem_jacobsonRadical` and `TauCeti.smul_mem_jacobsonRadicalSq`: over a linear
  category both are stable under the scalar action, which is what the submodule views record.

## Implementation notes

The membership condition is stated with the *categorical* composition `f ≫ g` and the identity
`𝟙 X`, not through the ring `CategoryTheory.End X`, whose multiplication is composition in the
opposite order. The two agree — `f ≫ g` is `g * f` in `End X` — but the ring spelling would make
the left-right asymmetry of the definition into an artefact of a convention rather than a genuine
symmetry to be proved.

Jacobson's lemma is proved as a private one-directional helper carrying the explicit inverse and
exposed as the equivalence `isIso_id_sub_comp_comm`; an instance form is impossible, since the two
hypotheses `IsIso (𝟙 X - a ≫ b)` and `IsIso (𝟙 Y - b ≫ a)` each imply the other and would loop.

The identification of `jacobsonRadical X X` with Mathlib's `Ring.jacobson (End X)` goes through
`TauCeti.Ring.mem_jacobson_iff_isUnit_one_add_mul_left`: `Ring.jacobson` is defined as the infimum
of the maximal left ideals, and Mathlib's quasi-regularity characterization of it
(`Ideal.mem_jacobson_bot`) is available only over a commutative ring, whereas `End X` is not
commutative. The noncommutative characterization is the one Tau Ceti proves for the left-right
symmetry of the radical, and it is exactly the defining condition used here.

The hypotheses `[IsLocalRing (End X)]` are the working form of "`X` is indecomposable": in a
Krull-Schmidt category the two are equivalent, and `IsLocalRing` carries the `Nontrivial`
hypothesis (`𝟙 X ≠ 0`) that rules out the zero object, on which every morphism is invertible and
the radical would be everything.

## References

* M. Auslander, I. Reiten, S. Smalø, *Representation Theory of Artin Algebras*, CUP (1995), V.7
  ("the radical of a category").
* I. Assem, D. Simson, A. Skowroński, *Elements of the Representation Theory of Associative
  Algebras, Vol. 1*, LMS Student Texts 65, CUP (2006), A.3 and IV.1.
* [Quiver-representations roadmap](https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCetiRoadmap/blob/main/TauCetiRoadmap/RepresentationTheory/QuiverRepresentations/README.md),
  Layer 6, sublayer 6A: "the radical of the module category and irreducible morphisms. ... The
  **radical** of the module category (maps that are non-isomorphisms between indecomposables) and
  its square; the irreducible maps are `rad / rad²`."
-/

public section

namespace TauCeti

open CategoryTheory

universe v u

variable {C : Type u} [Category.{v} C] [Preadditive C]

/-! ### Jacobson's lemma -/

/-- **Jacobson's lemma in a preadditive category**: if `𝟙 X - a ≫ b` is invertible, so is
`𝟙 Y - b ≫ a`, with inverse `𝟙 Y + b ≫ (𝟙 X - a ≫ b)⁻¹ ≫ a`.

This is the categorical form of the ring identity behind the left-right symmetry of the Jacobson
radical, and the reason the radical of a category can be defined by a one-sided condition. It is
the implementation of `TauCeti.isIso_id_sub_comp_comm`, which is the form to use. -/
private theorem isIso_id_sub_comp_swap {X Y : C} (a : X ⟶ Y) (b : Y ⟶ X)
    (h : IsIso (𝟙 X - a ≫ b)) : IsIso (𝟙 Y - b ≫ a) := by
  obtain ⟨w, hw₁, hw₂⟩ := h.out
  rw [Preadditive.sub_comp, Category.id_comp, Category.assoc] at hw₁
  rw [Preadditive.comp_sub, Category.comp_id] at hw₂
  -- `hw₁ : w - a ≫ b ≫ w = 𝟙 X` and `hw₂ : w - w ≫ a ≫ b = 𝟙 X`
  have key₁ : a ≫ b ≫ w ≫ a = w ≫ a - a := by
    have h₁ : a ≫ b ≫ w = w - 𝟙 X := by
      rw [eq_sub_iff_add_eq, ← hw₁]; abel
    have := congrArg (fun t : X ⟶ X => t ≫ a) h₁
    simpa [Preadditive.sub_comp] using this
  have key₂ : w ≫ a ≫ b ≫ a = w ≫ a - a := by
    have h₂ : w ≫ a ≫ b = w - 𝟙 X := by
      rw [eq_sub_iff_add_eq, ← hw₂]; abel
    have := congrArg (fun t : X ⟶ X => t ≫ a) h₂
    simpa [Preadditive.sub_comp] using this
  refine ⟨⟨𝟙 Y + b ≫ w ≫ a, ?_, ?_⟩⟩
  · simp only [Preadditive.sub_comp, Preadditive.comp_add, Category.id_comp, Category.comp_id,
      Category.assoc, key₁, Preadditive.comp_sub]
    abel
  · simp only [Preadditive.add_comp, Preadditive.comp_sub, Category.id_comp, Category.comp_id,
      Category.assoc, key₂, Preadditive.comp_sub]
    abel

/-- **Jacobson's lemma**, as an equivalence: `𝟙 X - a ≫ b` is invertible exactly when the
composite taken in the other order gives an invertible `𝟙 Y - b ≫ a`. -/
theorem isIso_id_sub_comp_comm {X Y : C} (a : X ⟶ Y) (b : Y ⟶ X) :
    IsIso (𝟙 X - a ≫ b) ↔ IsIso (𝟙 Y - b ≫ a) :=
  ⟨isIso_id_sub_comp_swap a b, isIso_id_sub_comp_swap b a⟩

/-! ### The radical -/

/-- **The radical of a preadditive category**, `rad(X, Y)`: the morphisms `f : X ⟶ Y` such that
`𝟙 X - f ≫ g` is invertible for every `g : Y ⟶ X`.

For `X = Y` this is the Jacobson radical of the ring `CategoryTheory.End X`, in its
characterization by quasi-regularity; the definition here is the extension of that condition to a
pair of objects, and it is a two-sided ideal of the category
(`TauCeti.comp_mem_jacobsonRadical_left`, `TauCeti.comp_mem_jacobsonRadical_right`). -/
def jacobsonRadical (X Y : C) : AddSubgroup (X ⟶ Y) where
  carrier := {f | ∀ g : Y ⟶ X, IsIso (𝟙 X - f ≫ g)}
  zero_mem' g := by simpa using (inferInstance : IsIso (𝟙 X))
  neg_mem' {f} hf g := by
    have := hf (-g)
    simpa [Preadditive.comp_neg, Preadditive.neg_comp] using this
  add_mem' {f₁ f₂} hf₁ hf₂ g := by
    have hu : IsIso (𝟙 X - f₁ ≫ g) := hf₁ g
    -- The composite `(𝟙 X - f₁ ≫ g) ≫ (𝟙 X - (inv (𝟙 X - f₁ ≫ g) ≫ f₂) ≫ g)` is the goal.
    have hswap : IsIso (𝟙 X - (inv (𝟙 X - f₁ ≫ g) ≫ f₂) ≫ g) := by
      rw [isIso_id_sub_comp_comm]
      have := hf₂ (g ≫ inv (𝟙 X - f₁ ≫ g))
      rw [isIso_id_sub_comp_comm] at this
      simpa [Category.assoc] using this
    have hfac : (𝟙 X - f₁ ≫ g) ≫ (𝟙 X - (inv (𝟙 X - f₁ ≫ g) ≫ f₂) ≫ g)
        = 𝟙 X - (f₁ + f₂) ≫ g := by
      rw [Preadditive.comp_sub, Category.comp_id, ← Category.assoc, ← Category.assoc,
        IsIso.hom_inv_id, Category.id_comp, Preadditive.add_comp]
      abel
    rw [← hfac]
    infer_instance

/-- **Membership in the radical**, spelled out: the defining condition, with the arbitrary
morphism `g` composed on the right of `f`. This is the interface of `TauCeti.jacobsonRadical`: it
is deliberately not a `simp` lemma, since unfolding every radical membership into a quantified
invertibility statement is not the normal form the consumers of the radical work in. -/
theorem mem_jacobsonRadical_iff_isIso_id_sub_comp_right {X Y : C} {f : X ⟶ Y} :
    f ∈ jacobsonRadical X Y ↔ ∀ g : Y ⟶ X, IsIso (𝟙 X - f ≫ g) :=
  Iff.rfl

/-- **The radical is left-right symmetric**: the defining condition may equally be read with the
arbitrary morphism `g` composed on the left of `f`, on the identity of `Y`. -/
theorem mem_jacobsonRadical_iff_isIso_id_sub_comp_left {X Y : C} {f : X ⟶ Y} :
    f ∈ jacobsonRadical X Y ↔ ∀ g : Y ⟶ X, IsIso (𝟙 Y - g ≫ f) :=
  ⟨fun hf g => (isIso_id_sub_comp_comm f g).1 (hf g),
    fun hf g => (isIso_id_sub_comp_comm f g).2 (hf g)⟩

/-! ### The radical is a two-sided ideal -/

/-- **Precomposing a radical morphism keeps it radical.** -/
theorem comp_mem_jacobsonRadical_left {W X Y : C} (h : W ⟶ X) {f : X ⟶ Y}
    (hf : f ∈ jacobsonRadical X Y) : h ≫ f ∈ jacobsonRadical W Y := by
  intro g
  rw [isIso_id_sub_comp_comm]
  simpa using mem_jacobsonRadical_iff_isIso_id_sub_comp_left.1 hf (g ≫ h)

/-- **Postcomposing a radical morphism keeps it radical.** -/
theorem comp_mem_jacobsonRadical_right {X Y Z : C} {f : X ⟶ Y}
    (hf : f ∈ jacobsonRadical X Y) (h : Y ⟶ Z) : f ≫ h ∈ jacobsonRadical X Z := by
  intro g
  rw [Category.assoc]
  exact hf (h ≫ g)

/-! ### On a single object: the Jacobson radical of the endomorphism ring -/

/-- **On a single object the radical is the Jacobson radical of the endomorphism ring**, in
membership form: `f : X ⟶ X` is radical exactly when it lies in `Ring.jacobson (End X)`.

The bridge is the elementwise description of the Jacobson radical of a possibly noncommutative
ring, `TauCeti.Ring.mem_jacobson_iff_isUnit_one_add_mul_left`: the defining condition below is
that description read through `CategoryTheory.End.mul_def`, the arbitrary `g : X ⟶ X` running over
the negatives of the arbitrary ring element. -/
theorem mem_jacobsonRadical_self_iff_mem_jacobson {X : C} {f : End X} :
    f ∈ jacobsonRadical X X ↔ f ∈ Ring.jacobson (End X) := by
  have key : ∀ y : End X, (1 : End X) + y * f = 𝟙 X - f ≫ (-y) := fun y => by
    rw [End.mul_def, Preadditive.comp_neg, sub_neg_eq_add, End.one_def]
  rw [Ring.mem_jacobson_iff_isUnit_one_add_mul_left,
    mem_jacobsonRadical_iff_isIso_id_sub_comp_right]
  refine ⟨fun h y => ?_, fun h g => ?_⟩
  · rw [key y, isUnit_iff_isIso]
    exact h (-y)
  · have h' := h (-g)
    rwa [key (-g), neg_neg, isUnit_iff_isIso] at h'

/-- **On a single object the radical is the Jacobson radical of the endomorphism ring.** This is
`TauCeti.mem_jacobsonRadical_self_iff_mem_jacobson` as an equality of subgroups, the form in which
`rad(X, X)` is handed to the ring theory of `End X`: the division ring by which Auslander-Reiten
theory divides is `End X ⧸ Ring.jacobson (End X)`. -/
theorem jacobsonRadical_self_eq_jacobson (X : C) :
    jacobsonRadical X X = (Ring.jacobson (End X)).toAddSubgroup :=
  AddSubgroup.ext fun _ => mem_jacobsonRadical_self_iff_mem_jacobson

/-! ### Radical morphisms are not invertible -/

/-- An invertible radical morphism forces the identity of its source to **vanish**: the defining
condition applied to the inverse says that `0` is invertible. -/
theorem id_eq_zero_of_isIso_of_mem_jacobsonRadical {X Y : C} {f : X ⟶ Y}
    (hf : f ∈ jacobsonRadical X Y) [IsIso f] : 𝟙 X = 0 := by
  have h : IsIso (𝟙 X - f ≫ inv f) := hf (inv f)
  rw [IsIso.hom_inv_id, sub_self] at h
  exact Limits.isIsoZeroSelfEquiv X h

/-- **A radical morphism out of an object with a nonzero identity is not an isomorphism.** -/
theorem not_isIso_of_mem_jacobsonRadical {X Y : C} {f : X ⟶ Y} (hX : 𝟙 X ≠ 0)
    (hf : f ∈ jacobsonRadical X Y) : ¬ IsIso f :=
  fun _ => hX (id_eq_zero_of_isIso_of_mem_jacobsonRadical hf)

/-! ### Objects with local endomorphism rings -/

section Local

variable {X Y : C} [IsLocalRing (End X)] [IsLocalRing (End Y)]

omit [IsLocalRing (End Y)] in
/-- **A morphism that is not a split monomorphism, out of an object with a local endomorphism
ring, is radical.** Only the source is constrained: were `f ≫ g` a unit of `End X` for some
`g : Y ⟶ X` then `g` composed with its inverse would be a retraction of `f`, so `f ≫ g` is a
nonunit and `𝟙 X - f ≫ g` is invertible by locality. -/
theorem mem_jacobsonRadical_of_not_isSplitMono {f : X ⟶ Y} (hf : ¬ IsSplitMono f) :
    f ∈ jacobsonRadical X Y := by
  intro g
  obtain ⟨e, he⟩ : ∃ e : End X, e = f ≫ g := ⟨f ≫ g, rfl⟩
  have hnu : ¬ IsUnit e := fun hu => by
    have : IsIso (f ≫ g) := he ▸ (isUnit_iff_isIso e).1 hu
    exact hf (IsSplitMono.mk'
      ⟨g ≫ inv (f ≫ g), by rw [← Category.assoc, IsIso.hom_inv_id]⟩)
  have hiso : IsIso ((1 : End X) - e) :=
    (isUnit_iff_isIso _).1 (IsLocalRing.isUnit_one_sub_self_of_mem_nonunits e hnu)
  rw [he] at hiso
  exact hiso

/-- **Between objects with local endomorphism rings the radical is the set of
non-isomorphisms.** This is the description of `rad(X, Y)` for indecomposable `X` and `Y` under
which it is used in Auslander-Reiten theory. It is the `simp` normal form of radical membership
in that setting, unlike the defining `TauCeti.mem_jacobsonRadical_iff_isIso_id_sub_comp_right`. -/
@[simp]
theorem mem_jacobsonRadical_iff_not_isIso {f : X ⟶ Y} :
    f ∈ jacobsonRadical X Y ↔ ¬ IsIso f := by
  constructor
  · intro hf hiso
    have := hiso
    exact one_ne_zero (α := End X) (id_eq_zero_of_isIso_of_mem_jacobsonRadical hf)
  · -- A morphism that splits is invertible here, its retraction turning `𝟙 X` into a composite.
    refine fun hf => mem_jacobsonRadical_of_not_isSplitMono fun hsm => hf ?_
    have := hsm
    -- Locality of `End Y` gives the trivial-idempotent hypothesis: an idempotent for the
    -- multiplication of `End Y` is one for composition, `CategoryTheory.End.mul_def`.
    have hY : ∀ e : Y ⟶ Y, e ≫ e = e → e = 0 ∨ e = 𝟙 Y := fun e he =>
      IsLocalRing.eq_zero_or_eq_one_of_isIdempotentElem (R := End Y)
        ((End.mul_def e e).trans he)
    exact isIso_of_isIso_comp (one_ne_zero (α := End X)) hY f (retraction f)
      (by rw [IsSplitMono.id]; infer_instance)

/-- **All morphisms between non-isomorphic objects with local endomorphism rings are radical.** -/
theorem jacobsonRadical_eq_top (h : IsEmpty (X ≅ Y)) : jacobsonRadical X Y = ⊤ := by
  refine eq_top_iff.2 fun f _ => mem_jacobsonRadical_iff_not_isIso.2 fun hf => ?_
  have := hf
  exact h.elim (asIso f)

omit [IsLocalRing (End Y)] in
/-- **An irreducible morphism out of an object with a local endomorphism ring is radical**, an
irreducible morphism never being a split monomorphism. -/
theorem IsIrreducibleMorphism.mem_jacobsonRadical {f : X ⟶ Y} (hf : IsIrreducibleMorphism f) :
    f ∈ jacobsonRadical X Y :=
  mem_jacobsonRadical_of_not_isSplitMono hf.not_isSplitMono

/-- **On a single object with a local endomorphism ring the radical is the set of non-units** of
that ring — the unique maximal ideal of `End X`, in the form available without commutativity. -/
theorem mem_jacobsonRadical_self_iff_not_isUnit {f : End X} :
    f ∈ jacobsonRadical X X ↔ ¬ IsUnit f := by
  rw [mem_jacobsonRadical_iff_not_isIso]
  exact not_congr (isUnit_iff_isIso f).symm

end Local

/-! ### The square of the radical -/

/-- **The square of the radical**, `rad²(X, Y)`: the subgroup of `X ⟶ Y` generated by the
composites `X ⟶ Z ⟶ Y` of two radical morphisms, over all objects `Z`.

The generating set is not itself a subgroup — a sum of two composites need not factor through a
single object without biproducts — so the closure is taken. -/
def jacobsonRadicalSq (X Y : C) : AddSubgroup (X ⟶ Y) :=
  AddSubgroup.closure {h : X ⟶ Y | ∃ (Z : C) (a : X ⟶ Z) (b : Z ⟶ Y),
    a ∈ jacobsonRadical X Z ∧ b ∈ jacobsonRadical Z Y ∧ h = a ≫ b}

/-- A composite of two radical morphisms lies in the square of the radical. -/
theorem comp_mem_jacobsonRadicalSq {X Z Y : C} {a : X ⟶ Z} {b : Z ⟶ Y}
    (ha : a ∈ jacobsonRadical X Z) (hb : b ∈ jacobsonRadical Z Y) :
    a ≫ b ∈ jacobsonRadicalSq X Y :=
  AddSubgroup.subset_closure ⟨Z, a, b, ha, hb, rfl⟩

/-- **The universal property of the square of the radical**: an additive subgroup contains
`rad²(X, Y)` exactly when it contains every composite of two radical morphisms.

This is the elimination rule matching the introduction rule
`TauCeti.comp_mem_jacobsonRadicalSq`; together they let containments of `rad²` be proved without
unfolding the closure that defines it, and it is the `simp` normal form of such a containment. -/
@[simp]
theorem jacobsonRadicalSq_le_iff {X Y : C} {K : AddSubgroup (X ⟶ Y)} :
    jacobsonRadicalSq X Y ≤ K ↔ ∀ (Z : C) (a : X ⟶ Z) (b : Z ⟶ Y),
      a ∈ jacobsonRadical X Z → b ∈ jacobsonRadical Z Y → a ≫ b ∈ K := by
  refine ⟨fun hle Z a b ha hb => hle (comp_mem_jacobsonRadicalSq ha hb), fun h => ?_⟩
  refine AddSubgroup.closure_le _ |>.2 ?_
  rintro _ ⟨Z, a, b, ha, hb, rfl⟩
  exact h Z a b ha hb

/-- **The square of the radical is contained in the radical**, the radical being closed under
composition. -/
theorem jacobsonRadicalSq_le_jacobsonRadical (X Y : C) :
    jacobsonRadicalSq X Y ≤ jacobsonRadical X Y :=
  jacobsonRadicalSq_le_iff.2 fun _ _ b ha _ => comp_mem_jacobsonRadical_right ha b

/-- **Precomposition preserves the square of the radical.** -/
theorem comp_mem_jacobsonRadicalSq_left {W X Y : C} (h : W ⟶ X) {f : X ⟶ Y}
    (hf : f ∈ jacobsonRadicalSq X Y) : h ≫ f ∈ jacobsonRadicalSq W Y := by
  induction hf using AddSubgroup.closure_induction with
  | mem x hx =>
    obtain ⟨Z, a, b, ha, hb, rfl⟩ := hx
    rw [← Category.assoc]
    exact comp_mem_jacobsonRadicalSq (comp_mem_jacobsonRadical_left h ha) hb
  | zero => rw [Limits.comp_zero]; exact zero_mem _
  | add x y _ _ hx hy => rw [Preadditive.comp_add]; exact add_mem hx hy
  | neg x _ hx => rw [Preadditive.comp_neg]; exact neg_mem hx

/-- **Postcomposition preserves the square of the radical.** -/
theorem comp_mem_jacobsonRadicalSq_right {X Y Z : C} {f : X ⟶ Y}
    (hf : f ∈ jacobsonRadicalSq X Y) (h : Y ⟶ Z) : f ≫ h ∈ jacobsonRadicalSq X Z := by
  induction hf using AddSubgroup.closure_induction with
  | mem x hx =>
    obtain ⟨W, a, b, ha, hb, rfl⟩ := hx
    rw [Category.assoc]
    exact comp_mem_jacobsonRadicalSq ha (comp_mem_jacobsonRadical_right hb h)
  | zero => rw [Limits.zero_comp]; exact zero_mem _
  | add x y _ _ hx hy => rw [Preadditive.add_comp]; exact add_mem hx hy
  | neg x _ hx => rw [Preadditive.neg_comp]; exact neg_mem hx

/-! ### The radical of a linear category -/

section Linear

variable {k : Type*} [Semiring k] [Linear k C]

/-- **Scaling a radical morphism keeps it radical**: in a linear category a scalar moves across a
composite, so scaling `f` has the same effect on the defining condition as scaling the arbitrary
morphism it is tested against. -/
theorem smul_mem_jacobsonRadical {X Y : C} (c : k) {f : X ⟶ Y}
    (hf : f ∈ jacobsonRadical X Y) : c • f ∈ jacobsonRadical X Y := fun g => by
  simpa using hf (c • g)

/-- **Scaling preserves the square of the radical**, the generating composites being scaled in
their first factor. -/
theorem smul_mem_jacobsonRadicalSq {X Y : C} (c : k) {f : X ⟶ Y}
    (hf : f ∈ jacobsonRadicalSq X Y) : c • f ∈ jacobsonRadicalSq X Y := by
  induction hf using AddSubgroup.closure_induction with
  | mem x hx =>
    obtain ⟨Z, a, b, ha, hb, rfl⟩ := hx
    rw [← Linear.smul_comp]
    exact comp_mem_jacobsonRadicalSq (smul_mem_jacobsonRadical c ha) hb
  | zero => rw [smul_zero]; exact zero_mem _
  | add x y _ _ hx hy => rw [smul_add]; exact add_mem hx hy
  | neg x _ hx => rw [smul_neg]; exact neg_mem hx

variable (k) in
/-- **The radical of a `k`-linear category as a submodule**, the same subgroup as
`TauCeti.jacobsonRadical` with its scalar action recorded. It is this view, and that of the square
below, that the quotient `rad(X, Y) / rad²(X, Y)` behind an arrow of the Auslander-Reiten quiver is
formed in. -/
def jacobsonRadicalSubmodule (X Y : C) : Submodule k (X ⟶ Y) where
  carrier := jacobsonRadical X Y
  add_mem' := add_mem
  zero_mem' := zero_mem _
  smul_mem' c _ hf := smul_mem_jacobsonRadical c hf

variable (k) in
/-- **The square of the radical of a `k`-linear category as a submodule**, the same subgroup as
`TauCeti.jacobsonRadicalSq`. -/
def jacobsonRadicalSqSubmodule (X Y : C) : Submodule k (X ⟶ Y) where
  carrier := jacobsonRadicalSq X Y
  add_mem' := add_mem
  zero_mem' := zero_mem _
  smul_mem' c _ hf := smul_mem_jacobsonRadicalSq c hf

/-- The submodule view of the radical has the same members as the subgroup. -/
@[simp]
theorem mem_jacobsonRadicalSubmodule {X Y : C} {f : X ⟶ Y} :
    f ∈ jacobsonRadicalSubmodule k X Y ↔ f ∈ jacobsonRadical X Y :=
  Iff.rfl

/-- The submodule view of the square of the radical has the same members as the subgroup. -/
@[simp]
theorem mem_jacobsonRadicalSqSubmodule {X Y : C} {f : X ⟶ Y} :
    f ∈ jacobsonRadicalSqSubmodule k X Y ↔ f ∈ jacobsonRadicalSq X Y :=
  Iff.rfl

/-- **The square of the radical is contained in the radical**, as submodules of a linear
category. -/
theorem jacobsonRadicalSqSubmodule_le_jacobsonRadicalSubmodule (X Y : C) :
    jacobsonRadicalSqSubmodule k X Y ≤ jacobsonRadicalSubmodule k X Y :=
  fun _ hf => jacobsonRadicalSq_le_jacobsonRadical X Y hf

end Linear

end TauCeti
